# ---- AI providers (same as before) ----
FAL_KEY=YOUR_FAL_KEY_ID:YOUR_FAL_KEY_SECRET
LLM_API_KEY=sk-proj-YOUR_OPENAI_PROJECT_KEY
LLM_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1
LLM_MODEL=gpt-4.1-mini

IMAGE_MODEL=fal-ai/nano-banana-2
IMAGE_EDIT_MODEL=fal-ai/nano-banana-2/edit

# ---- MIGRATED: Seedance 1.5 Pro -> Veo 3.1 Lite -> Kling 3.0 Turbo -> Veo 3.1 Lite -> Veo 3.1 FULL "Quality" tier -> Seedance 1.5 Pro (all via fal.ai, same FAL_KEY) ----
# REVERTED 2026-07-28: back to Seedance 1.5 Pro, the original provider this
# pipeline was built on, after the Veo Quality tier's $0.40/s led to a $50
# render that never finished. Seedance is ~8x cheaper (see
# USD_PER_VIDEO_SECOND below) and uses ONE endpoint for both i2v and flf
# shots -- an OPTIONAL end-frame field on the same call, not two separate
# endpoints the way Veo required. video.ts includes it only when the shot has
# an end keyframe -- nothing else in the pipeline needs to know this exists.
# TRIED SEEDANCE 2.5 — 2026-08-13/14, REVERTED. See video.ts's
# isSeedance25Model() / isSeedance25ReferenceModel() comments for the full
# history: BOTH the image-to-video and reference-to-video endpoints
# rejected an ordinary keyframe as a real-person likeness
# (content_policy_violation) — confirmed systemic across three separate
# ByteDance endpoints now (this pipeline's own prior Seedance 2.0 finding,
# plus both 2.5 endpoints), not one shot or one model version. Back to 1.5
# Pro. video.ts's 2.5 branches are left in place, untouched and inert.
# VIDEO_MODEL=bytedance/seedance-2.5/image-to-video
# VIDEO_MODEL=bytedance/seedance-2.5/reference-to-video
VIDEO_MODEL=fal-ai/bytedance/seedance/v1.5/pro/image-to-video
VIDEO_AUDIO=true
# Seedance genuinely supports 480p/720p/1080p (Veo was locked to 720p only —
# see src/config.ts). 480p chosen deliberately as the default: cheapest tier,
# while things stabilize after the $50 incident above.
VIDEO_RESOLUTION=480p

# ---- Cost model (see src/spend.ts) — the numbers checkCaps()/estimateShotsJob()
# actually forecast and enforce against. UNVERIFIED against a real invoice
# (see spend.ts's own comment) — check your fal.ai dashboard after the first
# real render and correct these if they differ. Previously undocumented here
# despite being read directly via process.env — this was the ONLY way to
# discover or tune the actual dollar-figure safety ceilings.
USD_PER_VIDEO_SECOND=0.052
USD_PER_IMAGE=0.04
# Hard ceilings — when either is hit, rendering STOPS (checkCaps() also now
# forecasts an upcoming job's own cost against these, not just spend already
# logged — see spend.ts). A blown cap is a bad day; an uncapped runaway is a
# bankruptcy. Defaults are deliberately conservative.
DAILY_SPEND_CAP_USD=500
USER_MONTHLY_CAP_USD=5000

# DELIVERY-RESOLUTION UPSCALING (see runtime.generationResolution / config.ts,
# providers/upscale.ts). When enabled, EVERY project generates on Seedance at
# 480p regardless of its own resolution setting -- the cheapest generation
# tier, always -- and a 720p or 1080p DELIVERY request is reached by
# upscaling the final accepted clip via a real video-upscaling model
# (fal-ai/topaz/upscale/video) instead of asking Seedance to render natively
# at that resolution. REAL, CONFIRMED integration: verified with a live test
# call against an actual Seedance 480p output (correct 2.25x pixel math,
# correct H264 output, correct duration, and a visually convincing result on
# direct frame inspection -- genuine added texture detail, not a blurry
# stretch). Only fires on shots that actually need it: a 480p delivery
# project never calls the upscaler at all (generation and delivery
# resolution are identical), and upscaling happens exactly once per shot,
# after QA has already settled on the final accepted take -- never on a
# re-rolled attempt that's about to be discarded anyway.
# DISCLOSED TO USERS ON PURPOSE (see safa-web's resolution picker copy) --
# this is a real, deliberate product decision, not something hidden from a
# paying customer. Set to false to revert to native per-tier generation
# (costs more per render at 720p/1080p, no upscale pass, no Topaz call).
UPSCALE_ENABLED=true
UPSCALE_MODEL=fal-ai/topaz/upscale/video
# Real, published Topaz pricing tiers, keyed by DELIVERY resolution (see
# spend.ts's costOfUpscale) -- not independently re-derived from a real
# invoice the way USD_PER_VIDEO_SECOND was, so verify against your own
# fal.ai dashboard after the first real upscale-tier render.
USD_PER_UPSCALE_SECOND_TO_720P=0.01
USD_PER_UPSCALE_SECOND_TO_1080P=0.02
USD_PER_UPSCALE_SECOND_ABOVE_1080P=0.08

SHOT_DURATION=6
MAX_DURATION=8
# Seedance's duration is NOT locked to a narrow enum the way Veo's was --
# confirmed step values are 4/6/8/10/12 seconds (not any integer). No more
# forced-8s-minimum on an end-framed shot the way Veo's flf endpoint had --
# src/providers/video.ts snaps to the nearest of that wider set, rounding
# DOWN on a tie (5->4, 7->6, 9->8, 11->10) so a clip never renders longer
# than planned.
# LONG-FORM CONSISTENCY: every this-many CHAINED shots in a row (i.e. within
# one long scene), 4-images.ts deliberately re-prioritizes the character sheet
# over the continuity chain for one shot -- pulling identity back toward the
# true reference instead of letting small per-shot drift keep compounding
# across a long scene. Matters much more for a 10-30 min film (scenes can run
# many shots) than for a 1-minute test. Lower = more frequent correction, more
# risk of a visible seam where identity "snaps back"; higher = smoother but
# more room for drift between corrections.
IDENTITY_REANCHOR_INTERVAL=5
# OFF by default. A dissolve blends the outgoing clip's last frames with the
# incoming clip's first frames for 350ms -- since every clip is an
# INDEPENDENTLY generated render (not real continuous footage), the character's
# pose never lines up between them, so that whole window shows two overlapping,
# misaligned copies of the same person. This is a deterministic (not
# probabilistic) source of a "merged/duplicate person" defect. Leave this off;
# only enable it after confirming a specific scene's consecutive poses actually
# align well enough that the dissolve doesn't ghost.
SCENE_DISSOLVE_ENABLED=false

# SCENE-TRANSITION DISSOLVE (see 6-assemble.ts's isGenuineTimeSkip() /
# shouldMerge()) -- a DIFFERENT feature from SCENE_DISSOLVE_ENABLED above, not
# a rename of it. That one dissolves within the SAME continuous scene (an
# angle change) and stays off for the ghosting reason documented on it. This
# one dissolves ONLY at a GENUINE detected time-skip between two shots -- a
# day/night flip, or an explicit "later that night"/"the next morning"-style
# phrase (the identical signal compiler.ts's own TIME_OF_DAY_JUMP_NO_SKIP
# check already validates) -- the classic, traditionally-motivated use of a
# dissolve as a passage-of-time cue. Same underlying xfadeMerge() cross-fade
# mechanism, so the same "two independently-rendered clips briefly
# superimposed" risk technically still applies, but practically much lower: a
# time-skip boundary is, by definition, a real scene change, so the two
# frames being blended are visually distinct rather than the same person's
# face in near-identical framing (what made the same-scene case read as a
# duplicate-person glitch). Mixing/truncation mechanics real-tested locally
# (mismatched-length synthetic clips, both directions) before shipping this
# default ON.
SCENE_TRANSITION_DISSOLVE_ENABLED=true

# SOFT-CUT AUDIO (see util.ts's softCutAssemble()) -- before this, EVERY
# boundary between scene runs (most cuts in a real film, since
# SCENE_DISSOLVE_ENABLED defaults off above) got concatClips()'s true,
# simultaneous hard stop/start on AUDIO too, not just picture. This gives
# audio a short in-place fade-to-meet at each boundary -- video stays a
# pixel-exact hard cut always, never blended, so none of SCENE_DISSOLVE_
# ENABLED's ghosting risk applies here.
# Short fade at a genuine scene change (different location/cast) -- just
# enough to avoid a click/pop, same "couple of frames" a real editor leaves
# even on a hard cut.
HARD_CUT_AUDIO_FADE_SEC=0.08
# Longer fade at a same-scene cut that ISN'T also getting a full visual
# dissolve (the common case) -- the honest approximation of a J-cut/L-cut's
# "linger" feel achievable with fixed-length clips that have no extra handle
# footage to borrow from (a TRUE overlapping pre-lap would need that).
SAME_SCENE_SOFT_CUT_FADE_SEC=0.30
# SOUND-MASKED CUTS -- if a cut already lands on a loud moment (a footstep, a
# slam, a line hitting hard), that transient already masks the seam, so the
# fade is shortened toward HARD_CUT_AUDIO_FADE_SEC instead of blunting it.
# Best-effort (ffmpeg astats measurement); never blocks assembly on failure.
SOUND_MASKED_CUTS_ENABLED=true

# FILM GRAIN (see util.ts's normalizeClip()) -- a real, deterministic ffmpeg
# noise pass applied to every clip during assembly, luma-only, so "subtle
# film grain" (already asked for in every keyframe prompt) is guaranteed
# present and consistent rather than depending on the generation model.
# UNVERIFIED strength -- kept deliberately mild (one published "vintage" ffmpeg
# recipe used 60; this is a fraction of that). Tune after a real render.
FILM_GRAIN_ENABLED=true
FILM_GRAIN_STRENGTH=6

# LENS VIGNETTE (see util.ts's normalizeClip()) -- real cameras naturally
# darken toward the frame corners; this makes the REALISM prompt's "shot on
# 35mm film with a real camera and lens" claim deterministically true.
# Visually reviewed: ffmpeg's own default angle (PI/5) reads as fairly
# noticeable; PI/6 here is the deliberately milder choice.
VIGNETTE_ENABLED=true
VIGNETTE_ANGLE=PI/6

# FINAL EXPORT ENCODE QUALITY (see util.ts's VIDEO_QUALITY_ARGS, applied to
# every libx264 encode: normalizeClip, buildTitleCard, applyEndFade,
# xfadeMerge). CONFIRMED REAL GAP: without these, every render fell back to
# libx264's implicit default (CRF 23, "medium" preset) and a hardcoded
# 128kbps audio bitrate — "web-good," not master-grade, with no tuning for
# the film grain/color grade this pipeline already adds on top. CRF 16 is
# visually-transparent tier (lower = higher quality/larger file); "slow"
# trades encode TIME for quality at zero extra cost, since this is a local,
# non-realtime export step. Previously undocumented here despite being read
# directly via process.env.
VIDEO_CRF=16
VIDEO_PRESET=slow
AUDIO_BITRATE=256k

# CINEMATIC LETTERBOX BARS (see util.ts's normalizeClip()) -- a pure stylistic
# choice, not a correctness fix like grain/vignette above. Paints black bars
# top and bottom, inside the existing frame (output dimensions don't change),
# approximating a widescreen ~2:1 look inside a 16:9 canvas. Only applies when
# the project's aspect ratio is 16:9 -- bars on an already-vertical 9:16 or
# square 1:1 frame would just crop into the image. TURNED ON 2026-08-02 at the
# user's own explicit request for a more cinematic look (was off by default
# since it's a style choice, not a correctness fix) -- set back to false to
# revert to a full-frame 16:9 delivery.
CINEMATIC_BARS_ENABLED=true
CINEMATIC_BARS_RATIO=0.115

# TITLE CARD (see util.ts's buildTitleCard() / 6-assemble.ts) -- the film's
# title, faded in and out on black, prepended before the first real clip.
# Default ON: unlike the bars above, a produced film with no title at all
# reads as "a pile of clips," same "absence reads as unproduced" reasoning as
# grain/vignette. Non-fatal by design -- if title-card generation fails for
# any reason (e.g. an unusual font/fontconfig setup) the render proceeds
# without it rather than failing. titleCardFont is a FONTCONFIG FAMILY NAME,
# not a file path -- real-tested to gracefully substitute a fallback font
# rather than error when the exact family isn't installed.
TITLE_CARD_ENABLED=true
TITLE_CARD_FONT=sans-serif
TITLE_CARD_DURATION_SEC=2.5

# END FADE (see util.ts's applyEndFade() / 6-assemble.ts) -- fades the fully
# assembled film to black/silence over its last N seconds. A hard stop on the
# last frame reads as "the render ended"; this is the one-line difference
# that makes it read as "the story ended" instead. Default ON, non-fatal.
END_FADE_ENABLED=true
END_FADE_DURATION_SEC=1.0

# LOUDNESS NORMALIZATION (see util.ts's normalizeClip()) -- every clip is an
# independent generation, so nothing otherwise guarantees consistent perceived
# volume shot to shot. -16 LUFS matches common streaming-video targets (not
# the quieter -24 LUFS broadcast standard, calibrated for a controlled
# listening environment this content won't have). CONFIRMED FRAGILE: real-
# tested against ffmpeg, this filter crashes the encoder on a perfectly
# digitally silent audio track -- normalizeClip() automatically retries
# without it if that happens, so a silent shot degrades gracefully rather
# than crashing the whole assembly.
LOUDNORM_ENABLED=true
LOUDNORM_TARGET_LUFS=-16

# INSTRUMENTAL SCORE (see providers/music.ts, util.ts's mixMusicBed(),
# 6-assemble.ts's finishFilm()) -- generates one instrumental score bed per
# film (fal.ai/cassetteai/music-generator, $0.02/output-minute -- negligible
# next to this pipeline's image/video spend) and mixes it under the film's
# existing dialogue/ambience track. DEFAULT OFF, UNLIKE every other cinematic
# flag in this file: this calls a real EXTERNAL PAID ENDPOINT this pipeline
# has never actually invoked. Its input/output shape is confirmed against
# fal's own OpenAPI schema (fetched directly -- the docs page itself 429'd,
# same rate-limit UPSCALE_MODEL's own comment already documents hitting) and
# fal's blog post announcing the model (confirmed instrumental-only output,
# not vocal songs), but NOT yet confirmed via a live test call the way
# UPSCALE_ENABLED's Topaz integration was before IT defaulted on. The MIXING
# side (ffmpeg amix/volume/afade) IS real-tested locally, against synthetic
# audio both longer AND shorter than the film, confirming correct truncation/
# silence-padding either way -- only the fal.ai call itself is unverified.
# Flip to true after running one real project and listening to the result --
# this is exactly the kind of thing that's easy to judge on a real render but
# impossible to calibrate blind, same discipline as FILM_GRAIN_STRENGTH/
# VIGNETTE_ANGLE above.
MUSIC_ENABLED=false
MUSIC_MODEL=cassetteai/music-generator
# Real, published per-MINUTE pricing (fal's own pricing page) — see
# spend.ts's costOfMusic(). Previously undocumented here despite being read
# directly via process.env.
USD_PER_MUSIC_MINUTE=0.02
# How far below the film's existing (dialogue + ambience) track the score
# sits, in dB. -18 is a conservative "clearly present but never competing
# with dialogue" starting point, NOT a real mixing-engineer decision -- there
# is no dynamic ducking keyed to actual speech here (that would need real
# per-shot voice-activity data this pipeline doesn't collect), just one
# constant attenuation for the whole bed. If dialogue ever feels crowded
# after a real listen, lower this further (more negative).
MUSIC_VOLUME_DB=-18
MUSIC_FADE_SEC=1.5
# DUCKING (see mixMusicBed() in util.ts) -- real sidechaincompress against the
# film's own baked audio track, not a separate VAD pass. Confirmed via fal's
# own current OpenAPI schema that Seedance has no separate dialogue/audio
# stream at all -- generate_audio is a bare boolean, output is one muxed
# file -- so there's no isolated speech signal to run true VAD against even
# in principle, and a VAD pass on the muxed mix would only approximate
# "something loud is happening" the same way sidechain energy already does.
# threshold is LINEAR amplitude (0-1, ffmpeg's own convention, not dB).
# Real-tested against synthetic audio: near-zero effect when the film is
# quiet, a real ~4-6dB reduction when it's loud -- confirmed correctly
# directioned. UNVERIFIED against a human ear on real dialogue -- tune after
# a real render.
MUSIC_DUCK_THRESHOLD=0.05
MUSIC_DUCK_RATIO=6

# SCENE AMBIENCE BED (see util.ts's synthesizeAmbienceBed()/mixAmbienceBed())
# -- OFF by default. Every clip's room tone is currently baked in
# independently by Seedance per shot, so it can shift subtly clip to clip
# even within one scene. This does NOT call any external generation model --
# no verified fal.ai sound-design/foley endpoint exists in this pipeline
# (cassetteai/music-generator is confirmed instrumental-music-only) -- it
# synthesizes a low-level filtered-noise texture per scene run, keyed off the
# same AMBIENCE_LIBRARY.json category compiler.ts already picks, and mixes it
# under that run's audio with the identical sidechain-ducking mechanism
# already proven for the instrumental score above.
AMBIENCE_BED_ENABLED=false
# Deliberately much quieter than MUSIC_VOLUME_DB -- meant to read as "the
# room has air in it," not as its own audible layer.
AMBIENCE_BED_VOLUME_DB=-30
AMBIENCE_BED_DUCK_THRESHOLD=0.05
AMBIENCE_BED_DUCK_RATIO=4

# BROADCAST-SPEC AUDIO EXPORT (see util.ts's exportBroadcastAudio()/
# upmixTo51()) -- real uncompressed 48kHz/24-bit LPCM WAV, loudness-
# normalized via an accurate 2-pass loudnorm. Spec CONFIRMED against
# Netflix's own current Partner Help Center page (fetched directly,
# 2026-08-02): "-27 LKFS +-2 LU, dialog-gated (ITU-R BS.1770), true peak
# <= -2 dBTP, 48kHz/24-bit LPCM". REAL-TESTED: independently re-measuring the
# actual function's output (not its own self-reported numbers) landed at
# -27.04 LUFS / -26.28 dBTP, confirmed genuine 24-bit PCM via ffprobe.
# ADDITIONAL artifact alongside the existing consumer MP4 (-16 LUFS AAC),
# never a replacement -- -27 LUFS would sound wrong as a default preview
# level. Off by default -- confirm by listening to a real export first, same
# discipline as every other new DSP feature in this file.
# NOTE ON SCOPE: this does NOT produce a spec-compliant IMF package (SMPTE
# ST 2067-21, JPEG2000 video in an MXF wrapper with CPL/PKL/OPL XML
# manifests) -- that is a fundamentally different deliverable type from this
# pipeline's consumer H.264/AAC MP4 architecture, requiring dedicated
# mastering/wrapping tooling this session did not attempt. What THIS flag
# produces is a broadcast-loudness-compliant LPCM audio stem, a real and
# useful component of a full delivery, not the full IMF package itself.
BROADCAST_EXPORT_ENABLED=false
BROADCAST_LOUDNESS_LUFS=-27
BROADCAST_TRUE_PEAK_DB=-2
# 5.1 upmix -- an HONEST technical upmix from stereo (L/R real signal,
# Center a derived sum, LFE/surrounds genuinely silent, not fabricated
# discrete surround content). Real-tested: confirmed via per-channel astats.
# Separate opt-in on top of BROADCAST_EXPORT_ENABLED.
BROADCAST_51_ENABLED=false

OPTIONS_COUNT=4
# Check your fal.ai dashboard's "Maximum concurrent requests" and match this
# to it exactly (or divide by however many worker processes you run at once,
# if more than one) -- set higher than the real account cap, the overflow
# gets rejected/queued by fal.ai instead of actually rendering.
CONCURRENCY=3
# How long ONE fal image call may hang before it is abandoned and retried.
# fal.subscribe() has no timeout of its own, so without this a stalled socket
# hangs the whole job. Was effectively 300s; lowered to 120s because a healthy
# call returns in seconds and a call still hanging at two minutes is stalled,
# not slow -- and providers/image.ts now retries a timeout IN PLACE (3 attempts)
# instead of letting it fail the entire job and re-run the whole step.
IMAGE_TIMEOUT_MS=120000
SCRIPT_PATH=scripts/sample.txt

# ---- Learned director rules (accumulated fixes, appended to every plan) ----
DIRECTOR_RULES_PATH=src/director/DIRECTOR_RULES.json

# ---- Post-render QA (vision model inspects each clip, re-rolls real failures) ----
# QA_MAX_RETRIES is a real cost lever: every retry is a full paid re-render (2x
# with audio on).
QA_ENABLED=true
QA_MAX_RETRIES=1
# A duplicated/merged person, a blurry frame, unnatural motion, a flat performance,
# identity drift, and a treadmill (no real progress) are dealbreakers, not ordinary
# defects -- users forgive a slightly stiff walk but not the lead appearing twice,
# a face that's become someone else, or a "run" that goes nowhere. These CRITICAL_KINDS
# (see src/providers/qa.ts) get their own LOWER trigger threshold (0.3 vs the normal
# 0.5).
# HISTORY, KEPT HONEST: this budget has gone 3 -> 1 -> 3 -> 1. First cut to 1
# on the reasoning that several root causes had been fixed at the source (the
# assembly-stage dissolve that superimposed misaligned frames, the stale-
# artifact cache, weak multi-person cast-lock text) -- a real render then
# directly contradicted that (13 of 14 shots still failed QA even after their
# one retry), so it was restored to 3. Cut back to 1 later with real evidence
# from a DIFFERENT angle: on a 7-shot film, only 1 shot was ever actually
# rescued by a retry -- the other 6 exhausted all 3 attempts with critical
# severity staying flat or getting WORSE, and different defects appearing on
# different attempts (not the signature of noise a fresh seed fixes, more
# retries buys little). If you hit the same "N of M shots still fail even
# after retrying" pattern again, that's a sign the ROOT CAUSE (prompt/scene
# complexity/reference quality) needs fixing, not the retry count raised
# again -- this number alone has already been tuned back and forth twice.
QA_CRITICAL_MAX_RETRIES=1
QA_SEVERITY=0.5
QA_FRAMES=6
QA_VISION_MODEL=gpt-4.1-mini
# QA CALL RELIABILITY (see providers/qa-runtime.ts) — a SEPARATE retry budget
# from QA_MAX_RETRIES/QA_CRITICAL_MAX_RETRIES above, which govern re-rendering
# after a CONFIRMED content defect. This one governs retrying the QA
# INFRASTRUCTURE CALL ITSELF after a transient failure (a network blip, a
# vision-model timeout) — without it, ANY transient QA error used to silently
# return "pass: true", indistinguishable from a genuine pass. After these
# attempts are exhausted, the shot is flagged unverified (never silently
# marked as passing). Previously undocumented here despite being read
# directly via process.env.
QA_CALL_MAX_ATTEMPTS=3
QA_CALL_RETRY_DELAY_MS=1500

# ---- Director/breakdown reasoning model ----
# The ONE call that plans the entire film from the script. This is where "logic and
# story coherence" is won or lost. Two real options, a genuine SPEED-vs-QUALITY
# tradeoff, not a free choice:
#   - gpt-5.6-sol (or gpt-5.6-terra, mid-tier) -- a REASONING-tier model, does
#     internal "thinking" before answering. Best story logic/causal-chain/continuity
#     planning, but confirmed the single slowest call in the whole pipeline.
#   - gpt-4.1 -- non-reasoning, notably faster. Set here when total generation time
#     matters more than squeezing out the last bit of planning quality; watch a few
#     breakdowns for weaker causal-chain/continuity plans if you switch to this.
# GPT-5-family models reject a custom `temperature` in Chat Completions; the code
# detects this by name and omits it automatically either way.
#
# "Text tokens are pennies" stopped being true once the system prompt grew domain
# packs and a LEARNED DIRECTOR RULES file (src/director/DIRECTOR_RULES.json, and it
# only grows over time) -- that whole prompt is now several thousand tokens, resent
# on every call.
BREAKDOWN_MODEL=gpt-4.1

# ---- Repair-loop model (THE cost lever if breakdown is getting expensive) ----
# repairShots() is a DIFFERENT job: it applies pre-written, prescriptive fixes
# ("give this beat two endpoints", "add a notice-cue beat") to a small subset of
# already-flagged shots -- mechanical text edits against an explicit rulebook, not
# open-ended planning. It can run up to MAX_REPAIRS times per film, EACH TIME paying
# for the same big system prompt on whatever model this is set to. If BREAKDOWN_MODEL
# is a flagship/reasoning-tier model, leaving repairs on that same model is usually
# the single biggest avoidable cost in the whole pipeline.
# Defaults to BREAKDOWN_MODEL (zero behaviour change if left unset). Point it at a
# cheaper/faster model once you've spot-checked repaired shots still look right --
# the compiler re-validates every repair afterward regardless, so a bad repair gets
# caught on recompile, not shipped silently. regenerateShot() (single-shot "give me
# a different take") uses this same model, for the same reason.
#
# CONFIRMED REAL FAILURE MODE with a flagship model here: repairShots()'s context
# includes the FULL shot list every call (not just the flagged batch), and
# directorReadThrough()/checkShotVariety() below are single monolithic calls with
# no splitting at all -- REPAIR_MAX_INPUT_TOKENS's batch-splitting only helps when
# a batch has more than one shot, so a single already-large shot (or a mid-length
# film's read-through) can exceed a flagship model's per-minute token ceiling on
# its own. That's a hard per-request limit, not a frequency problem -- it will
# 429 every single time, not intermittently, and silently fall back to LLM_MODEL.
# A real render surfaced exactly this: every repair/read-through/variety call for
# a 10-shot film hit a 30K TPM ceiling and fell back to gpt-4.1-mini 100% of the
# time. gpt-4.1-mini is the safer default here for that reason -- set this to a
# flagship model only once you've confirmed your account's TPM tier actually
# covers a full-film repair/read-through request, not just a single-shot one.
REPAIR_MODEL=gpt-4.1-mini
# SAFETY CAP for a single repair request. OpenAI's per-minute token limit is a HARD
# ceiling on ONE request, not a "wait and it clears" throttle -- a request bigger than
# the account's TPM limit for that model will 429 with "Request too large" every
# single time. When a repair round has many flagged shots, the batch is split until
# each request's estimated size fits under this number.
# RAISED to 90000 (2026-08-06) -- CONFIRMED REAL: the old 24000 default was sized
# for REPAIR_MODEL=gpt-4.1's ~30K TPM ceiling, but REPAIR_MODEL has been
# gpt-4.1-mini (a materially higher-TPM tier) since 2026-08-04, and this cap was
# never recalibrated after that switch -- forcing repair batches to pre-emptively
# split into several sequential calls far more often than the account can
# actually handle, a real, confirmed contributor to slow (15-20 min) breakdowns.
# Real gpt-4.1-mini calls in this codebase's history have already succeeded at
# 27-30K tokens. Low-risk to raise: a wrong guess just falls back to today's
# split-and-retry behavior (isRequestTooLargeError catch in repairShots()), never
# a hard failure. Lower back toward 24000 if real 429s appear instead of graceful
# splitting.
REPAIR_MAX_INPUT_TOKENS=90000

# ---- Director's read-through (one extra paid LLM call per render) ----
# After the compile-repair loop already produced a blocker-free shot list, this
# reads the WHOLE film once more like a director watching an animatic -- not
# checking shots against the rulebook (the compiler already did that), but
# judging the SEQUENCE: motivation, whether the geography/timing actually holds
# together across shots, whether a beat is padding. Uses REPAIR_MODEL. Set to
# "false" to skip it and save that call.
DIRECTOR_REVIEW_ENABLED=true

# ---- TESTING ONLY — do not set in a real production deployment ----
# Forces the free-plan watermark onto EVERY render, including paid/enterprise
# plans (see worker.ts's handleAssemble()) — for pre-launch testing/ad
# material on a paid test account, where every render should carry the mark
# regardless of plan. A real paying enterprise customer would never have this
# set. Leave unset/false in production. Previously undocumented here despite
# being read directly via process.env.
FORCE_WATERMARK=false

# ---- Shared database (SAME Neon URL as the web app) ----
# `pgbouncer=true` is REQUIRED on Neon's pooled endpoint — without it Prisma's
# prepared-statement handling doesn't play well with a transaction-mode
# pooler, and things can grow slower under load until they time out.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@YOUR-PROJECT-pooler.REGION.aws.neon.tech/DBNAME?sslmode=require&channel_binding=require&pgbouncer=true"

# ---- Cloudflare R2 (S3-compatible object storage) ----
R2_ENDPOINT="https://YOUR_ACCOUNT_ID.r2.cloudflarestorage.com"
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID="YOUR_R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID"
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY="YOUR_R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY"
R2_BUCKET="YOUR_BUCKET_NAME"
# Public base URL for the bucket (enable public access / r2.dev, or a custom domain)
R2_PUBLIC_BASE="https://YOUR_PUBLIC_R2_DOMAIN"
